b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1033 2548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050677 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1031 "Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1033 6367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050895 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6f396a30-c21c-5a35-935d-80293083bda2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150167 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf8068aa-dbbd-5b69-9937-8b4236d86198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150169 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150169 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150169 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec064a4d-7102-57fc-b186-402daf8b164b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150170 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "189c918e-7d05-5308-8e1d-edf7c3216627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150171 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150171 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150171 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c66de07-d217-52ea-a332-34af866e7b87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151905 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151905 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151905 #edb/ref 1074 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/63/63e7eee4a168f339b9dfb1fb69ca67a3747d567020ade08c2bbab3121b530b72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151906 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151906 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151906 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151906 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/57/573b2182d03d7e15927368117c3e610cc018dbcda0711754083a2d8a3c95320d.svg)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151907 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151907 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151907 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151907 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151908 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151908 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151908 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152277 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152278 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152279 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152280 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152305 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "32095b5a-ea4a-5f14-a569-3cb501bb8677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd30eee5-3768-5759-bb64-72e553a332ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152610 #edb/ref 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153267 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153267 #edb/ref 1061 593388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153267 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e3b73d3-8c02-5744-a1ae-6c50e8804ed2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153267 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153268 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153268 #edb/ref 1061 593389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153268 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be6dde9b-9d0b-585b-a6b3-25eff080cc7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153268 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153269 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153269 #edb/ref 1061 593392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153269 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77116319-3453-5eec-ac47-013a9a68819f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153269 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153270 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153270 #edb/ref 1061 593393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153270 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba9f7dc5-2fb4-5f3b-8a80-2aa8c6b90d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153270 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153271 #edb/ref 1061 593397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25bf33fb-6ffa-53ff-952f-c30347b7e55c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153271 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153272 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153272 #edb/ref 1061 593400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153272 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d45e0175-eb26-56f3-9d0a-ff5fb71127aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153272 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153273 #edb/ref 1061 593401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153273 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "817b1fa5-bf11-5056-811f-2154d660e047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153273 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153328 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n![](images/57/57c395afa6eecd7289b91a25e61b5310447cc3702daf019b0046615242f34057.svg)\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153328 #edb/ref 1065 14497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153328 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ede0bd1e-1d97-5029-bbb3-a95bbadc7c88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153328 #edb/ref 1067 "The Wronskian" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153329 #edb/ref 1064 "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153329 #edb/ref 1065 14498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153329 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1733749-f7ac-5c62-80aa-f971e91c66d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153329 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets of Fundamental Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153330 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$  \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153330 #edb/ref 1065 14499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153330 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d8e0478-c6f1-57b4-b41b-1fe464cb7fdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153330 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305324 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305353 #edb/ref 1080 "2{x}^{3}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16996d4c-2b6c-5166-8105-980226657e7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305485 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}-x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305485 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305485 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2315271b-6c07-5281-bbfe-463dcd686f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305860 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305860 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305860 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "490c271b-9e2e-51db-a620-2f1605eefe48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305990 #edb/ref 1080 "dependent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5