96877-d442-5c94-acec-552c52c92c49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307992 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94aa5ac7-2d4a-5d7f-9c4e-6fb13a1afc3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308039 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2c02124-a667-54aa-af69-f5fffebd4c39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308050 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a548607e-4ac1-535f-9c60-003214855934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308124 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bf71c9df-84b5-5944-afd6-3194ac26fbd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a747558-5780-50c9-a35b-84cd8e00bad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308562 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308590 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421245 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421245 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421245 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c7fbe1-9c5b-5cb0-b4a6-9f060759a858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421246 #edb/ref 1080 "{k}_{1}+{k}_{2}=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421246 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421246 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421247 #edb/ref 1080 "2{k}_{1}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0" #edb/ref 